Work backlog item PL-16 — continue converting `Project` cast sites to the shared
builder. Two families already closed; `Project` stands at 134 and is all that is
left.

WHAT DONE MEANS. Every one of the 134 sites has been READ and dispositioned, so
that: converted + must-stay = 134, and the ledger's `Project` ceiling equals the
must-stay count. Reaching ZERO is not the target and is not expected — some
sites legitimately cannot convert. EXHAUSTION is the target.

A previous run set the condition as "the ceiling is lower than it starts", which
was satisfied by the first batch and ended the goal after three. Lower is not
done. Do not stop because the number moved.

HOW TO PROVE IT EACH TURN. After every batch run
`npx jest --no-coverage tests/sop/canonical-fakes.test.ts > /tmp/fakes.txt 2>&1`
and paste the `Project` ceiling. Also paste a running count: "converted N,
must-stay M, unread R" where N+M+R = 134. The evaluator reads only what you
surface, so an unpasted number does not exist.

THE TRAP. `createMockProject` supplies rich defaults and several suites assert on
the ABSENCE of fields. A blind substitution typechecks, passes, and silently
changes what the test means. Before converting a site, read what the SUBJECT
consumes and what the test asserts. If it turns on a missing or empty field,
classify it must-stay and record the file and the reason — that is a result, not
a failure.

RULES. Stay on the current work branch; never checkout or merge develop. Lower
ceilings only. One `Backlog: PL-16` commit per batch, each gated on
`npm run gate` exiting 0 with the exit code captured in a variable, never read
through a pipe. Push after each commit. Touch nothing under `src/`. No cloud
writes.

FINISH BY WRITING THE MUST-STAY LIST into the backlog item as a dated section —
every file, with the one-line reason it cannot convert. That list is the real
deliverable; the ceiling is just its count.

Stop after 40 turns, or earlier if two consecutive batches fail the gate for the
same reason.
